-Parsing vs. String Manipulation: Never try to parse a CSV or JSON file manually using string splits or regular expressions. Always use your programming language's built-in libraries (like Python's csv and json modules). They automatically handle edge cases like commas wrapped in quotes or nested brackets.

-Character Encoding: Files are saved using specific encodings (how binary bits turn into letters). Always standardise on UTF-8 encoding to avoid strange character corruption bugs (like the dreaded `` symbol).

-Streaming Large Files: Loading a 5GB CSV or JSON file entirely into memory will crash your application. Learn to stream or read files line-by-line (or chunk-by-chunk) when handling large datasets.

-Validation: JSON files can be validated against a "JSON Schema" to ensure incoming data matches the exact format, types, and required fields your application expects.


Composition
inheritance means that specific thing has every single properties of the perent class(is a)
-So no features of the parent class can go wrong for the child class
-for eample riza is a human - then ria will have heart property of the parent class human
-But composition is used for a (has a ) relationship.For example, car has an engine(the engine can be diesel, petrol based on the type). so we add those feautres individually and add thm up under a class.So modification is also easy unlike inheritance which has stricter rules

Low Level Design
Nouns - classes
its attributes and methods- think about what that specific noun like restaurant is and what it can do
Relationship - figure out what is connected to what to perform an action and how they are connected

State and invariants
-state holds the data at that specific instancs 
 for eg : balance = 500 it can get updated or deducted in the next operation
-invariants:
this are the business or logicall rules that should not be violated if the system has to function
for eg : the balance should be lesser than 500 , if the transaction results in that block that transaction(the account should have 500 minimum balance)


virtual evironments
-one virtual environment fro one project
-python -m venv env
-.env\Scripts\activate.bat
-pip install libraries and dependencies(those will stay inside the ve itself)
-prepare arequirements.txt with specific versions for other software engineers who might work on this project
      when they are using your project by cloning it:they run:-
           pip install -r requirements.txt 
      this install all the libraries and dependencies mentioned in requirements.txt
-add the venv to .gitignore so its not uploaded to github
-deactivate

libary : the actual code
dependency : how my project related to it
   The moment your project imports a library because your code depends on it to function, that library officially becomes a dependency of your project
-r : in the command it says Hey pip, look inside the text file following    
this flag, read it line by line, and install every library listed inside it.




my-python-app/                 <-- Root Directory (Git repository)
├── .github/                   <-- CI/CD workflow automation (e.g., GitHub Actions)
├── .vscode/                   <-- VS Code local editor workspace settings
│   └── settings.json          <-- Forces VS Code to use the local venv
├── docs/                      <-- Project manuals and API documentation
├── src/                       <-- Source Code Directory
│   └── my_app/                <-- Main package name (use underscores, not hyphens)
│       ├── __init__.py        <-- Makes this directory an importable module
│       ├── main.py            <-- Entry point to execute the application
│       ├── config.py          <-- Loads environment variables & constants
│       ├── database.py        <-- Database connection and session setup
│       ├── models.py          <-- Data definitions (SQLAlchemy, Pydantic, etc.)
│       └── utils.py           <-- Global reusable helper functions
├── tests/                     <-- Test suite directory (Mirrors src structure)
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── conftest.py            <-- Global testing configurations/fixtures for pytest
│   ├── test_main.py           <-- Tests for main.py logic
│   └── test_models.py         <-- Tests for models.py data structures
├── .env                       <-- REAL local secrets (DB passwords, API keys)
├── .env.example               <-- TEMPLATE for secrets (DUMMY values only!)
├── .gitignore                 <-- Tells Git what to ignore (venv, .env, __pycache__)
├── pyproject.toml             <-- Modern Python build configuration & tool settings
├── README.md                  <-- Markdown onboarding guide for developers
├── requirements.txt           <-- Simple direct dependency list for pip
└── venv/                      <-- Local virtual environment (sandbox runtime)

-__init__.py : so we just hold the main address of the importable module inside iniy.py under smaller    names so we just have to import using that small name in our code and do t worry about that long path

-.env .example : env files with values replaced with fake values. so if a deveeloper downloads it they fill 
  in their own values and continue
  we commit this to github

-.gitignore :  A text file listing names of files and folders that Git should completely ignore tracking.
  eg: venv/, .env, __pycache__

-README.md: (for anyone landing on your github project)
       brief context about the app,
       exact terminal setup steps
       how to run tests
       explanation of configuration options.

-venv/:
        The localized virtual environment folder containing the project's isolated Python interpreter executable and downloaded dependency binaries.

-docs/:(for developers)
        A folder dedicated purely to human-readable manuals, system design flowcharts, and technical markdown specifications describing how the system works conceptually

-config.py:
       the trusted manager file which deals with en directly.Other files approach this file for the env variables.It prechecks before passing on the varibale

-database.py:
       database connections, engine instances and connection pools

-models.py:
       define structures , classes, orms etc

       
-utils.py:
     helper functions are defined here

-capsys(in-built tool of pytest):
    watch terminal window during test
